<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharnaces (son of Arsames)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 565–497 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Parnaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pharnaces was a member of the Achaemenid family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, a son of Arsames and younger brother of Hystaspes, and so an uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. According to the fortification tablets found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, he was the chief economic official of Darius between 506 and 497 BCE, a Mayor of the Palace whose attribute was a short stick, probably of a precious metal; he likely appears on some of the Persepolis reliefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> records that he introduced mules into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespontine-phrygia">Hellespontine Phrygia</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. His son Artabazus was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of the region by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> around 477 BCE, and Artabazus's heirs, the Pharnacid dynasty, ruled it into the 4th century BCE, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took it over.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharnaces-son-of-arsames.jpg" alt="Pharnaces (son of Arsames)" width="330" height="718" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Davey from Portland, Oregon, EE UU · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pharnaces.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
